As an alternative to individually replacing tasks, you can redefine the whole main loop, by overriding `Application::initializeMainLoopTasks[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a11370.html#af0cae36f638bca64a7e771431f8c584c)`. Kanzi attaches the default main loop tasks in this function. To allow copying, its implementation is intentionally exposed in the header.
[![../../_images/initializemainlooptasks.svg](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/initializemainlooptasks.svg)](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/initializemainlooptasks.svg)
### Adding timers[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/application-configurations/application-configuration.html#adding-timers "Link to this heading")
When you want Kanzi to execute a task at regular time intervals, you can add the task before a stage of the main loop using `MainLoopScheduler::prependTimer[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a13618.html#aa9e5613cffcb29c8d4255d201353e94c)` and after a stage of the main loop using `MainLoopScheduler::appendTimer[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a13618.html#a80a817ce7c52ac8cd37799f8f2bd20d0)`.
Use timer recurrence options to execute a timer only once (`MainLoopScheduler::TaskRecurrence::OneTime[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a13618.html#aba0f144351f8dd75db0fc404e05546d5aef01a357e0efe7452a8aaf4fcf9df1c8)`) or repeatedly (`MainLoopScheduler::TaskRecurrence::Recurring[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a13618.html#aba0f144351f8dd75db0fc404e05546d5a582ff616b727759a2208c65b27b0d309)`). When a recurring timer elapses multiple times in a single iteration of the Kanzi main loop, Kanzi executes that timer only once.
C++

```
using TimerTask = function<void(chrono::nanoseconds, unsigned int)>;

```
Copy to clipboard
Java

```
public interface TimerTask {
    void handle(Duration elapsedDuration, int repeatCount);
}

```
Copy to clipboard
Kotlin

```
interface TimerTask {
    fun handle(elapsedDuration: Duration?, repeatCount: Int)
}

```
Copy to clipboard
When Kanzi executes a timer, it passes as arguments:
  * The elapsed duration rounded down to a multiple of the interval of the timer
  * The number of elapsed intervals


For example, to update time on a clock using a recurring timer:
C++

```
void activateClock()
{
    // Add a recurring timer task that updates the clock once every second.
    m_clockTimer = getMainLoopScheduler().appendTimer(UserStage,
                                                      kzMakeFixedString("Clock"),
                                                      MainLoopScheduler::TimerRecurrence::Recurring,
                                                      chrono::seconds(1),
                                                      [this](auto elapsedDuration, auto repeatCount)
                                                      {
                                                          this->updateClock(elapsedDuration * repeatCount);
                                                      });
}

void deactivateClock()
{
    // Remove the clock timer.
    getMainLoopScheduler().removeTimer(m_clockTimer);
}

```
